            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "With https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/phosphor-state-manager/+/78827 coming, does that mean there will be 2 events every time a BMC reboots?  Is that excessive?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "We might end up with 3 events:\n\n- \"BMC Rebooted because of ...\"\n- \"BMC is NotReady\"\n- \"BMC is Ready\"\n\nI don't really see that as a problem but I don't know how to rationalize what someone might see as \"excessive\" vs someone else.  That's why we have the ignore-list, right?\n\nEvents aren't suppose to be problems.  Knowing when the BMC is Ready externally is fairly useful though.  If we're managing a huge set of servers, let's say through a firmware update sequence, the Ready event (being a push notification from the BMC via Redfish) can tell external services that the BMC is back up and running after the reboot (post update) and now the next firmware item can be updated.  Having these events is pretty useful for sequencing."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Matt Spinler",
                        "email": "spinler@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "spinler"
                    },
                    "message": "Fair enough."
                }
            ],
